speechtext:
Mr. Speaker,
it is not the established practice to publish correspondence which is of a confidential nature between the government of Canada and governments of other countries, nor between the government and its representatives abroad, especially where the correspondence relates to matters which affect other countries or to matters that are pending. However, knowing the desire of hon. members to have the fullest information possible with respect to matters bearing immediately upon the Italo-Ethiopian dispute, and particularly with reference to the happenings at Geneva referred to by my hon. friend in his motion, I indicated some days ago that I would , ask the Under Secretary of State for External Affairs to have prepared as speedily as possible a 
white paper containing the essential documents bearing on these matters. I hope to be able before the end of the present week to table a white paper which will contain the following documents:
The report of the committee of the council on the facts of the dispute and the recommendations made by the council in September for settlement, with the replies of Italy and Ethiopia ;
The action taken in the council and in the assembly following these reports;
The setting up of the coordination committee and the committee of eighteen, with their recommendations;
The steps taken by the Canadian government with regard to these recommendations; and
A statement prepared by the coordination committee stating which members of the League of Nations have or have not taken action on the various sanctions proposals, the December peace proposals, etcetera.
The statement will also include the committee discussion on proposal 4(a)-the oil sanction proposal-regarding extension of the embargo on exports to Italy.
I think these documents will cover practically all that my hon. friend and other members of the house will wish to have. If, however, anything has been inadvertently omitted which should be included in the white paper, I shall endeavour, when the time comes, to make good the omission so far as it is in keeping with the established practice.
